WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.942 --> 00:00:03.900
So Thank you, everyone.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.860
My name is David Smith and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.210
at Microsoft.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:11.722
And if you've ever given a
tutorial using R or just

00:00:11.722 --> 00:00:13.430
tried to share R
code with other people

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.830
and gotten these
kind of responses,

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:17.362
then this talk is for you.

00:00:17.362 --> 00:00:19.070
Now you might get
these kind of reactions

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.560
because somebody didn't
read the setup instructions

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.880
or installed the
wrong version of R.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.550
And so your code
doesn't work or they're

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:27.675
using a different operating
system than you expect,

00:00:27.675 --> 00:00:29.990
or just using an old
laptop that can't

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:32.509
cope with the data and the
code that you provided.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:34.190
But you can resolve
all of these issues

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.870
by running in something
called GitHub CodeSpaces.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.380
So let's see how this works.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.860
I've got here a repository,
which you can find on GitHub.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.860
It has everything you
need to recreate what

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.053
I'm showing you right here.

00:00:44.053 --> 00:00:45.470
But one of the
things that it does

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:48.890
include is a .devcontainer
folder, and that

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.590
is the magic that allows you
to click on that code button

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.470
directly in GitHub and then run
the code within CodeSpaces.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.030
Now this is running
in real time.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.850
It takes about 10 or 20 seconds.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.480
What GitHub is doing right here
is setting up a CodeSpace,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.670
kind of like a little
virtual machine that

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.010
has the version of
R that I specified

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.080
installs all the R package
versions that I specified.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.430
It also installs python, my
data, my code, everything

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.500
is all right there,
ready for anybody

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.590
who clicks on the button to
get exactly in the environment

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:22.220
that I intended for somebody
to run my code within.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.160
So once that CodeSpace launches
in just a couple of seconds,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.230
what we'll see is
a web interface

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.880
that looks much like
Visual Studio Code.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.410
And in fact, this is Visual
Studio Code running directly

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:34.560
within the browser.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.080
And I can do all
the usual things

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.800
like browsing within my system
that comes from my repository.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.450
This is actually a
tutorial that comes

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.810
from a collection provided by
Microsoft, including a Jupyter

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.670
notebook, which is
to teach somebody

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.250
how to do data
exploration using R.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.860
So there's notebook has popped
up directly within Visual Studio

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.840
Code and I can
interact with it as you

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:54.930
would any other notebook.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.990
Like I can run this
cell right here.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.390
And the surprising thing is
that runs without any errors,

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.060
without any setup, because
all of those packages

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.560
are already preinstalled
within the CodeSpace.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.190
So your participants can then
interact with the notebook,

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:08.130
run the code.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.789
Here's a tidyverse function
which is reading in a data file

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:12.270
and showing in the
first few lines.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.330
It's a very, very
natural experience,

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.850
regardless what kind of
laptop that they brought

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.490
to the workshop with them.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:22.370
But what if you wanted to run
your workshop within RStudio

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.120
instead of Visual Studio Code?

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.670
That's easy too, because we
have RStudio Server installed

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.250
within this CodeSpace.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.000
There's need to go to the ports
tab in Visual Studio Code,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.360
and that launches RStudio Server
directly within my browser.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.480
So this is the same as
RStudio on the desktop,

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.180
just running on a browser and
I can use that to open files.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.610
And again, I'm going to open
a file from my repository.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.460
They happen to live in
the workspaces folder

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:47.910
when it's running on the server.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.760
Then I browse through
that and this time

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.710
let's grab an RMarkdown
document instead.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.400
So we can see how that
works within CodeSpaces.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.740
Now we have R Markdown
documents and we can just

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.410
use the usual knit
function within RStudio

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.868
to Knit it to HTML and we can
see our output really quickly

00:03:03.868 --> 00:03:05.660
because it's running
on a machine with lots

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.350
of RAM, lots of CPU.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.190
So these computations
happen nice and fast.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.700
This is all thanks to the menu.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.790
The magic rather
of dev containers,

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.590
which is a standardized
way of setting up

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.060
a container for
interactive use like this.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.900
You saw that dev container
folder in my repository.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.690
That's where I specify the
R versions, the packages

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:25.220
and everything
else that I needed

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.070
to give the same experience
for every user interacts

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:31.820
with my repo. Dev containers, by
the way, are 100% open source

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:32.803
technology.

00:03:32.803 --> 00:03:34.220
There is a command
line interface,

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.050
so you can integrate
those dev containers

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.270
into any application,
not just GitHub,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:39.860
and you can find
lots of information

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.525
about them at containers.dev

00:03:42.525 --> 00:03:44.900
But just to give you a little
bit of a flavor of how that

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.910
works, if you're
familiar with containers,

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:48.590
you know about
Docker files where

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.270
you can specify the
software that gets

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.020
installed into that container.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.590
In this particular case, I'm
grabbing a prebuilt container

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.140
from the rocker project
and awesome project

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:59.300
providing containers for R.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.200
I chose the version
of R in this case,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.150
it's grabbing the
latest 4.x version.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.390
So I got the very
newest 4.2.1

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.310
for that particular
example and I'm

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.510
installing a bunch of packages
into that container as well.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:13.010
There's another file that lets
you configure Visual Studio

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.100
Code, set some options if
people are using that editor

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.630
and you can also
specify commands

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.399
to run at the beginning
of the container,

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:21.290
like starting the
RStudio S erver,

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:22.940
which is how we
got that to work.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.060
So in summary, if you want to do
a tutorial or share some code,

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.700
just create a GitHub
repo as you always do.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.950
Put all your scripts, data,
notebooks and instructions

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:34.010
into there, but then set
it up as a dev container

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.050
so that all the software
to run your code

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.660
is fixed and ready for
anybody to use simply

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.810
by clicking on that "Run in
GitHub CodeSpaces" button

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.260
directly in GitHub.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.800
Now GitHub CodeSpaces is
only available right now

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.680
within GitHub Enterprise
and GitHub Education.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.160
If your participants don't have
access to CodeSpaces today,

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.968
they can run out locally
using Visual Studio Code.

00:04:54.968 --> 00:04:57.260
And I have another talk which
shows you how that works,

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.140
which you can't quite see, but
you can find that link directly

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:01.203
on my GitHub repository that

00:05:01.203 --> 00:05:02.620
you see on your
screen right there

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.912
with everything you need to
make all this work for you.

00:05:04.912 --> 00:05:06.480
Thank you.

